[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Gradle Build System & Custom Task Creation in Android (Java)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Gradle build system is the backbone of modern Android development. It manages dependencies, compiles Java source code, runs tests, and packages your final APK or App Bundle. Understanding how to extend Gradle by writing custom tasks allows you to automate repetitive development workflows in your Android projects."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Gradle Build Lifecycle"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before creating custom tasks, it is crucial to understand that Gradle executes builds in three distinct phases:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"1. Initialization Phase: Gradle determines which projects and modules are taking part in the build and creates a Project instance for each."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"2. Configuration Phase: All project build scripts are executed. Tasks are created, configured, and their dependency graphs are wired."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"3. Execution Phase: Gradle executes the subset of tasks created and configured during the previous phase, based on the requested command-line arguments."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a Simple Custom Task in build.gradle"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can define custom tasks directly inside your app-level build.gradle (or build.gradle.kts) file. Here is how you can write a basic task using Java/Groovy syntax that logs build information."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"tasks.register('printBuildInfo') {
    group = 'custom'
    description = 'Prints current build configuration details.'
    doLast {
        println('=== Android Build Information ===')
        println('Project Name: ' + project.name)
        println('Compile SDK: ' + android.compileSdk)
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the code above, the 'doLast' block ensures that the code inside executes strictly during the Execution Phase, rather than prematurely during the Configuration Phase."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Executing the Custom Task"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"To run your newly created custom task, open your terminal at the root of your Android project and execute the following Gradle wrapper command:"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"./gradlew printBuildInfo"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Advanced Custom Task: Automating Asset Generation"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"A common use case in Android is generating a version properties file or cleaning specific directories before compilation. Here is a custom task that generates a build timestamp file inside your assets folder."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"task generateTimestampAsset {
    group = 'custom'
    description = 'Generates a timestamp file into the assets directory.'

    def assetsDir = file("$projectDir/src/main/assets")

    outputs.dir(assetsDir)

    doLast {
        if (!assetsDir.exists()) {
            assetsDir.mkdirs()
        }
        def timestampFile = new File(assetsDir, "build_timestamp.txt")
        timestampFile.text = "Build generated at: " + new Date()
        println("Timestamp successfully written to " + timestampFile.absolutePath)
    }
}

// Hook the custom task into the standard Android build pipeline
tasks.named('preBuild') {
    dependsOn('generateTimestampAsset')
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"By adding 'dependsOn("generateTimestampAsset")' to the standard 'preBuild' task, we guarantee that our custom timestamp generator runs automatically every time an Android build is initiated."}]
